  "about": "T2.1a's type map (design, choice 2), written by the package's independent test author before the implementation, recorded by contract/tools/type_map_record.py: the one definition of a decimal's canonical text (plain positional notation, no exponent, no trailing fractional zeros, no trailing point, -0 written 0), which the Python loader and the Rust key binders each implement and both run; a SQLite decimal stored in a column declared TEXT as that text, since a column declared DECIMAL takes numeric affinity and rounds what it stores (sqlite_distinct_keys, KO2-35); and equal keys of different scales that must still join on every engine (join_across_scales, lesson 19). `map` is the stored type per engine, with Databricks' and Snowflake's added by T3.3 (design, choice 1): an unsigned integer declared in a type holding its range, and a type an engine has no column type for stored in a form of its own (`<engine>_value`), listed and never counted as covered (KO2-47); Lakebase is Postgres; the rest of the type set's entries are the implementer's, added here under review.",
